The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!extended_tag_field_enable) {
    // Use of 8-bit Tags
}
```
```c
// Certain Broadcom devices do not handle 8-bit Tags correctly
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because certain Broadcom devices (HT1100 and HT2000) do not handle 8-bit Tags correctly as Completers when the Extended Tag Field Enable is not set. This violation of the PCIe specification leads to issues with peer-to-peer DMA operations, resulting in system hangs and unresponsive behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0xHT1100, quirk_no_ext_tags);
```
```c
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0xHT2000, quirk_no_ext_tags);
```
Reason:
The fix involves adding quirks for the affected Broadcom devices to disable Extended Tags for the entire hierarchy. This ensures that the system does not attempt to use 8-bit Tags with these devices, thereby preventing the issues with peer-to-peer DMA and resolving the hangs and unresponsive behavior in Xorg and keyboard functionality.

